WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.552
A café keeps a record of five days

2
00:00:05.552 --> 00:00:06.840
the temperature

3
00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:09.493
and how many iced coffees it sold

4
00:00:09.493 --> 00:00:10.781
in tens of cups.

5
00:00:10.781 --> 00:00:12.903
Measured from the averages

6
00:00:12.978 --> 00:00:15.252
the five days make this little

7
00:00:15.328 --> 00:00:16.540
cloud of points.

8
00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:19.537
Their covariance matrix C has five

9
00:00:19.615 --> 00:00:22.962
and two on the diagonal, the two variances

10
00:00:22.962 --> 00:00:26.232
and two off the diagonal, the covariance.

11
00:00:26.232 --> 00:00:27.555
It is symmetric

12
00:00:27.633 --> 00:00:30.047
flipping it across the diagonal

13
00:00:30.124 --> 00:00:31.370
changes nothing.

14
00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:35.007
Here is what C does to the unit circle.

15
00:00:35.007 --> 00:00:37.788
It turns it into a long ellipse

16
00:00:37.788 --> 00:00:40.400
lying along the cloud of days.

17
00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:43.357
The two eigenvectors of C

18
00:00:43.357 --> 00:00:46.165
at what angle do they meet?

19
00:00:46.165 --> 00:00:47.870
Pause and guess.

20
00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:54.359
Trace seven, determinant six

21
00:00:54.359 --> 00:00:57.670
the eigenvalues are six and one.

22
00:00:57.670 --> 00:01:00.077
Six goes with two, one

23
00:01:00.077 --> 00:01:03.087
the long axis of the ellipse

24
00:01:03.087 --> 00:01:05.795
warm days with more sales.

25
00:01:05.795 --> 00:01:08.805
One goes with one, minus two

26
00:01:08.805 --> 00:01:10.410
the short axis.

27
00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:14.527
Their dot product is two minus two: zero.

28
00:01:14.527 --> 00:01:17.100
They meet at a right angle

29
00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:20.040
like the axes of every ellipse.

30
00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:23.164
That picture is the theorem.

31
00:01:23.255 --> 00:01:25.708
Every real symmetric matrix

32
00:01:25.799 --> 00:01:27.434
does three things.

33
00:01:27.525 --> 00:01:30.614
Turn the plane so the eigenvectors

34
00:01:30.705 --> 00:01:32.158
lie on the axes.

35
00:01:32.158 --> 00:01:35.520
Stretch each axis by its eigenvalue

36
00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:36.701
six and one.

37
00:01:36.701 --> 00:01:37.700
Turn back.

38
00:01:38.050 --> 00:01:41.947
In symbols: S equals Q lambda Q transpose.

39
00:01:41.947 --> 00:01:46.029
The columns of Q are the unit eigenvectors

40
00:01:46.029 --> 00:01:47.606
at right angles

41
00:01:47.606 --> 00:01:51.410
so undoing the turn is just Q transpose.

42
00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:54.879
There is a second way to read it.

43
00:01:54.879 --> 00:01:56.367
Take a vector x.

44
00:01:56.367 --> 00:01:59.081
Project it onto the long axis

45
00:01:59.081 --> 00:02:01.270
and onto the short axis.

46
00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:04.976
Then S x is six times the first piece

47
00:02:05.067 --> 00:02:07.425
plus one times the second.

48
00:02:07.516 --> 00:02:11.053
A symmetric matrix is a weighted sum of

49
00:02:11.144 --> 00:02:14.500
projections onto perpendicular lines.

50
00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:16.929
Why perpendicular?

51
00:02:17.031 --> 00:02:20.112
Take eigenvectors x and y with

52
00:02:20.215 --> 00:02:24.014
different eigenvalues, lambda and mu

53
00:02:24.014 --> 00:02:28.225
and compute x transpose S y in two ways.

54
00:02:28.225 --> 00:02:32.538
Grouping S with y gives mu times x dot y.

55
00:02:32.538 --> 00:02:34.489
Grouping S with x

56
00:02:34.489 --> 00:02:37.365
using S transpose equals S

57
00:02:37.365 --> 00:02:40.240
gives lambda times x dot y.

58
00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:42.468
The eigenvalues differ

59
00:02:42.468 --> 00:02:45.161
so the dot product must be zero.

60
00:02:45.161 --> 00:02:48.345
Symmetry is used in exactly one place.

61
00:02:48.426 --> 00:02:51.610
A similar argument with complex numbers

62
00:02:51.691 --> 00:02:54.630
shows that the eigenvalues are real.

63
00:02:55.210 --> 00:02:58.414
Without symmetry the picture breaks.

64
00:02:58.414 --> 00:03:02.241
Lecture eighteen's matrix, four, one, two

65
00:03:02.241 --> 00:03:04.467
three, is not symmetric.

66
00:03:04.467 --> 00:03:08.205
It also turns the circle into an ellipse

67
00:03:08.205 --> 00:03:11.765
but its eigenvectors, one, one and one

68
00:03:11.765 --> 00:03:15.770
minus two, are not the axes of that ellipse.

69
00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:19.287
They meet at an angle whose cosine is

70
00:03:19.373 --> 00:03:22.026
minus zero point three one six

71
00:03:22.026 --> 00:03:24.080
far from a right angle.

72
00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:27.159
So, for a symmetric matrix

73
00:03:27.159 --> 00:03:30.860
are any two eigenvectors perpendicular?

74
00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:33.886
Not when an eigenvalue repeats.

75
00:03:33.886 --> 00:03:37.436
The simplest case is two times the identity.

76
00:03:37.436 --> 00:03:40.355
It stretches every direction by two

77
00:03:40.355 --> 00:03:43.116
so every vector is an eigenvector.

78
00:03:43.116 --> 00:03:44.615
One, zero and one

79
00:03:44.615 --> 00:03:46.746
one are both eigenvectors

80
00:03:46.746 --> 00:03:48.560
at forty five degrees.

81
00:03:48.910 --> 00:03:51.897
The theorem only says that perpendicular

82
00:03:51.971 --> 00:03:53.987
eigenvectors can be chosen.

83
00:03:53.987 --> 00:03:56.675
Here, pick one, zero and zero, one.

84
00:03:56.675 --> 00:03:57.945
NumPy's e i g h

85
00:03:57.945 --> 00:04:00.334
written for symmetric matrices

86
00:04:00.334 --> 00:04:02.350
makes that choice for you.

87
00:04:02.930 --> 00:04:05.114
One symmetric matrix is a

88
00:04:05.201 --> 00:04:08.084
favourite of computers: a mirror.

89
00:04:08.171 --> 00:04:10.704
Take a unit vector u and form

90
00:04:10.792 --> 00:04:13.063
I minus two u u transpose.

91
00:04:13.063 --> 00:04:15.072
It sends u to minus u

92
00:04:15.159 --> 00:04:17.605
and keeps every vector along

93
00:04:17.693 --> 00:04:19.090
the mirror line.

94
00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:20.767
It is symmetric

95
00:04:20.767 --> 00:04:23.918
and reflecting twice changes nothing

96
00:04:23.918 --> 00:04:26.073
so it is also orthogonal.

97
00:04:26.073 --> 00:04:29.224
Put the mirror halfway between three

98
00:04:29.224 --> 00:04:30.966
four and five, zero.

99
00:04:30.966 --> 00:04:33.204
They have the same length

100
00:04:33.204 --> 00:04:35.360
so the mirror swaps them.

101
00:04:35.710 --> 00:04:39.060
A rotation can do the same job: turn three

102
00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:41.007
four down onto the axis.

103
00:04:41.007 --> 00:04:42.487
Mirrors and turns

104
00:04:42.487 --> 00:04:44.902
clearing one column at a time

105
00:04:44.902 --> 00:04:47.083
give the Q R factorization

106
00:04:47.083 --> 00:04:50.510
and they stay orthogonal to the last digit.

107
00:04:50.860 --> 00:04:53.315
Classical Gram Schmidt does not.

108
00:04:53.392 --> 00:04:55.463
On columns that differ only

109
00:04:55.540 --> 00:04:57.228
in the eighth decimal

110
00:04:57.304 --> 00:04:59.683
it returns q's at sixty degrees

111
00:04:59.759 --> 00:05:01.140
instead of ninety.

112
00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:02.586
To sum up.

113
00:05:02.586 --> 00:05:04.835
A symmetric matrix turns

114
00:05:04.835 --> 00:05:07.951
stretches along perpendicular axes

115
00:05:07.951 --> 00:05:09.335
and turns back.

116
00:05:09.422 --> 00:05:12.624
It sends a circle to an ellipse whose

117
00:05:12.710 --> 00:05:14.960
axes are the eigenvectors.

118
00:05:15.310 --> 00:05:17.512
Its eigenvalues are real

119
00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:19.977
and its eigenvectors can be

120
00:05:20.065 --> 00:05:21.914
chosen perpendicular.

121
00:05:22.002 --> 00:05:25.701
And mirrors and rotations build orthogonal

122
00:05:25.789 --> 00:05:28.430
matrices that stay orthogonal.

123
00:05:29.010 --> 00:05:30.097
In part two

124
00:05:30.097 --> 00:05:33.538
what if every eigenvalue is positive?

125
00:05:33.538 --> 00:05:37.613
Then the matrix is called positive definite

126
00:05:37.613 --> 00:05:40.330
and it is everywhere in data.
